{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ואמרתי חי אנכי", :translit "va'omar chai anochi", :gloss "And I said: As I live"} {:he "לעלם", :translit "la'olam", :gloss "forever"} {:he "לעלם כתיב חסר וי\"ו", :translit "la'olam katuv chaseir vav", :gloss "la'olam is written defective without vav"} {:he "לאחר ששת אלפים שנה", :translit "le'achar sheshet alafim shanah", :gloss "after six thousand years"} {:he "שאז יהיה נשגב ה' לבדו", :translit "she'az yihyeh nisgav Hashem levado", :gloss "for then Hashem alone will be exalted"} {:he "ונחסר וי\"ו", :translit "venechsar vav", :gloss "and the vav will be removed"} {:he "שששה ימים נדונים הרשעים", :translit "sheishesha yamim nidonim harsha'im", :gloss "for six days the wicked are judged"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "לעלם written chaser (defective) without vav hints to the limit of 'forever' as six millennia"}], :frame {:text "The word לעלם is written without a vav, signaling that the sworn existence is bounded. The tradition that this world endures for six thousand years, after which God alone is exalted, is found in Sanhedrin 97a and is identified there as a teaching of the Tanna of the school of Eliyahu. The equation of a thousand years to a day appears in Psalms 90:4 ('for a thousand years in Your sight are but as yesterday'). The phrase 'the Lord alone shall be exalted' on that day is the language of Isaiah 2:17. The notion that the wicked are punished for six millennia draws on the same thousand-years-per-day schema.", :sources ["Sanhedrin 97a" "Psalms 90:4" "Isaiah 2:17"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "לעלם written chaser (defective) without vav hints to the limit of 'forever' as six millennia", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:41:07.486124736Z", :model "glm-5.2"}